{"name":"Jugendstil Turquoise","hex":"#5f9b9c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"jugendstil","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The German equivalent of art nouveau."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/jugendstil"},{"term":"turquoise","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A sky-blue, greenish-blue, or greenish-gray semi-precious gemstone.","A pale greenish-blue colour, like that of the gemstone."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of turquoise (the gemstone).","Having a pale greenish-blue colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/turquoise"}],"entries":[{"title":"Jugendstil","blocks":[{"text":"Jugendstil was an artistic movement, particularly in the decorative arts, that was influential primarily in Germany, Austria, and elsewhere in Europe to a lesser extent from about 1895 until about 1910. It was the German and Austrian counterpart of Art Nouveau. The members of the movement were reacting against the historicism and neo-classicism of the official art and architecture academies. It took its name from the art journal Jugend, founded by the German artist Georg Hirth. It was especially active in the graphic arts and interior decoration."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Mathildenhoehe-ernst-ludwig-haus-076.jpg/330px-Mathildenhoehe-ernst-ludwig-haus-076.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jugendstil","wikidata":"Q1294605"},{"title":"Turquoise","blocks":[{"text":"Turquoise is an opaque, blue-to-green mineral that is a hydrous phosphate of copper and aluminium, with the chemical formula CuAl6(PO4)4(OH)8·4H2O. It is rare and valuable in finer grades and has been prized as a gemstone for millennia due to its hue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Turquoise-40031.jpg/330px-Turquoise-40031.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turquoise","wikidata":"Q165254"},{"title":"Turquoise (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Turquoise is a cyan color, based on the mineral of the same name."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turquoise_(color)","wikidata":"Q5960345"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Otto_Eckmann_-_Jugend_Nr._14%2C_1896.jpg/500px-Otto_Eckmann_-_Jugend_Nr._14%2C_1896.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Otto_Eckmann_-_Jugend_Nr._14%2C_1896.jpg/500px-Otto_Eckmann_-_Jugend_Nr._14%2C_1896.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOtto_Eckmann_-_Jugend_Nr._14%2C_1896.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/La_maison_de_Peter_Behrens_%28Mus%C3%A9e_de_la_colonie_d%27artistes%2C_Darmstadt%29_%288728647639%29.jpg/500px-La_maison_de_Peter_Behrens_%28Mus%C3%A9e_de_la_colonie_d%27artistes%2C_Darmstadt%29_%288728647639%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/La_maison_de_Peter_Behrens_%28Mus%C3%A9e_de_la_colonie_d%27artistes%2C_Darmstadt%29_%288728647639%29.jpg/500px-La_maison_de_Peter_Behrens_%28Mus%C3%A9e_de_la_colonie_d%27artistes%2C_Darmstadt%29_%288728647639%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALa_maison_de_Peter_Behrens_(Mus%C3%A9e_de_la_colonie_d'artistes%2C_Darmstadt)_(8728647639).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Fauteuil_et_d%C3%A9cors_de_porte_de_Peter_Behrens_%28Mus%C3%A9e_de_la_colonie_d%27artistes%2C_Darmstadt%29_%288729765938%29.jpg/250px-Fauteuil_et_d%C3%A9cors_de_porte_de_Peter_Behrens_%28Mus%C3%A9e_de_la_colonie_d%27artistes%2C_Darmst